From cbf56acdfb31e8b1f479aba35cc7e11d0f57a372 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 07:10:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add accessibility needs to guests step and restructure budget
 into 4 price-only tiers

Two configurator changes, both end-to-end (wizard, DB, admin, matching):

Accessibility: new section in the guests step ("does anyone in your group have specific accessibility needs?" -> Yes/No, then multi-select of mobility/visual/hearing/cognitive/other needs with a free-text field for "other"). Stored as lead.accessibility ({needs: string[], otherDetails}) on both leads and Proposal.proposal_data. Experiences get 5 new nullable booleans (accessibility_*) plus an admin notes field, editable in FiltersCard as an explicit Not specified/Yes/No control (not a checkbox) so 'never set' stays distinguishable from 'not accessible' -- existing experiences default to neutral, never penalized. generateProposal.ts scores (not filters) on this: +70 if an experience declares compatibility with a need the lead has, -70 if it explicitly declares incompatibility, 0 if unset -- deliberately not a hard exclusion, per spec.

Budget: replaced the Essential/Signature/Luxury labels (site_copy keys deleted) with 4 plain price tiers (200-500/500-1000/1000-3000/3000+), stored as a structured key (e.g. "500_1000") instead of the old free-text range string. Added BUDGET_TIERS as the single source of truth (experienceTaxonomy.ts) driving the wizard, the admin checkboxes (now 4, extended from 3, same opt-in pattern) and matching. generateProposal.ts's 3 duplicated budget-comparison blocks now go through one helper; on top of the existing hard filter, added a price-relevance scoring bonus/penalty based on the experience's real base_price relative to the requested tier, so a correctly-priced experience outranks one that's only nominally flagged for that tier.

Admin leads (list + detail) and internal notification emails now show a formatted budget label and the declared accessibility needs. Verified the new matching logic against the exact scenarios from the spec (price-proximity scoring, hard filter on the new tier, accessibility bonus/penalty/neutral) with an isolated test -- all pass. Backfilled budget_200_500 for existing experiences whose base_price already falls in that range, so the new tier isn't empty on day one; the other 3 tiers' existing admin-curated checkboxes are untouched.
